    "suggested_action": "human_review",
    "reason": "The English lemma 'back' (noun) has several common meanings, including the human body part (e.g., 'my back hurts') and the rear part/direction of an object or space (e.g., 'the back of the car'). The Japanese expression '後方' (こうほう) primarily refers to the rear part or direction ('rear', 'behind') and does not encompass the meaning of the human body part. Therefore, '後方' is semantically narrower than the general English noun 'back'. A general learner would expect to learn a word like 背中 (senaka) for the human body part 'back', which is a very common usage. Aligning 'back' generally to '後方' would lead to incorrect usage for this common sense.",
